Generate an OWL 2 DL ontology fragment in Turtle for the database table described below.

Use these namespace prefixes (already declared — do not redeclare):
  @prefix ind:  <{ontology_prefix}> .
  @prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
  @prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
  @prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
  @prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
  @prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .

[CONTEXT]
Table name (PascalCase for class):   {table_class_name}
Source table identifier:              {table_name}

Database schema:
{schema_context}

Relevant documentation:
{documents}

Existing ontology concepts (candidates for reuse — reference by full IRI where appropriate):
{existing_ontology}

Core ontology generated so far in this run (avoid redefining these):
{core_ontology}

[INSTRUCTIONS]
1. Declare an owl:Class `ind:{table_class_name}` for the table with rdfs:label and rdfs:comment.
2. For each column, decide:
   - If the column participates in a FOREIGN KEY constraint, declare an owl:ObjectProperty with rdfs:range set to the referenced class IRI (use ind:<ReferencedClassPascal>).
   - Otherwise, declare an owl:DatatypeProperty with an rdfs:range set to the most appropriate xsd datatype (xsd:integer, xsd:string, xsd:decimal, xsd:dateTime, xsd:boolean, xsd:float, xsd:double). NEVER use xsd:date — it is not in the OWL 2 datatype map and will be rejected by DL reasoners; use xsd:dateTime instead.
3. For every property:
   - Set exactly one rdfs:domain = ind:{table_class_name}.
   - Set exactly one rdfs:range.
   - Add rdfs:label (a human-readable camelCase name) and rdfs:comment (description).
   - Add prov:wasDerivedFrom <{provenance_base}/{table_name}/COLUMN_NAME>.
4. Reuse existing concepts from the context block above when they semantically match. When reusing, use their full IRI rather than creating a new one under ind:.
5. Do not create properties named "is", "has" alone, or other generic stubs — always use meaningful names derived from the column semantics.
6. Do not duplicate classes or properties already present in the core ontology so far.
7. Forbidden constructs — do NOT emit any of these (they cause parser failures or are silently discarded downstream):
   - Blank nodes in any position (e.g. `[ ... ]`, `[]`).
   - owl:Restriction, owl:onProperty, owl:minCardinality, owl:maxCardinality, owl:cardinality.
   - Inline property lists nested beyond one level (no `[ ] , [ ]` chains).
   Emit only flat triples: `<subject> <predicate> <object> .`
8. Class completeness: if you reference any class IRI in rdfs:domain or rdfs:range that is NOT already in the core ontology above, you MUST declare it with `a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "..." .` in this fragment. Do not leave referenced classes undeclared.
9. Foundational grounding: when the existing ontology concepts block above shows a class with similarity ≥ 0.85, declare your induced class as a subclass: `ind:YourClass rdfs:subClassOf <matched_foundational_iri> .` This grounds your class in the existing vocabulary.

Output only valid Turtle. Do not include an @prefix block (those are already declared). Do not wrap in markdown fences. Do not add explanatory text before or after the Turtle.
